     2	Research Artifacts in Secondary Studies: A Systematic Mapping in Software
     3	Engineering
     4	Aleksi Huotalaa, Miikka Kuutilab, Mika Mäntyläa
     5	aUniversity of Helsinki, Helsinki, Finland
     6	bDalhousie University, Halifax, Canada
     7	Abstract
     8	Context:Systematic reviews (SRs) summarize state-of-the-art evidence in science, including software engineering
     9	(SE).Objective:Our objective is to evaluate how SRs report research artifacts and to provide a comprehensive list
    10	of these artifacts.Method:We examined 537 secondary studies published between 2013 and 2023 to analyze the
    11	availability and reporting of research artifacts.Results:Our findings indicate that only 31.5% of the reviewed studies
    12	include research artifacts. Encouragingly, the situation is gradually improving, as our regression analysis shows a
    13	significant increase in the availability of research artifacts over time. However, in 2023, just 62.0% of secondary
    14	studies provide a research artifact while an even lower percentage, 30.4% use a permanent repository with a digital
    15	object identifier (DOI) for storage.Conclusion:To enhance transparency and reproducibility in SE research, we
    16	advocate for the mandatory publication of research artifacts in secondary studies.
    17	Keywords:Research artifact, Replication, Reproducibility, Repeatability, Open science, Open research, Open data,
    18	FAIR data
    19	1. Introduction
    20	Systematic reviews (SRs) summarize state-of-the-art
    21	evidence in software engineering (SE). Artifact research
    22	in SE has previously focused on replicating experi-
    23	ments [1] and increasing reproducibility in technical ar-
    24	eas of software engineering [2]. SRs should include a
    25	research artifact with all necessary details for several rea-
    26	sons.
    27	Reason 1: Replicability (Reproducibility or Repeata-
    28	bility). Replication is a hallmark of science. Other re-
    29	searchers must be able to replicate the review process to
    30	confirm its reliability and accuracy. These issues are pro-
    31	moted in open science [3], open data [4], and in the princi-
    32	ples of Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, and Reusable
    33	(FAIR) data endorsed in Nature in 2016 [5].
    34	Reason 2: Trust. Research artifacts build trust in the
    35	integrity of the evidence. When the methods, data, and
    36	process are fully available, readers and practitioners cantrust that the conclusions are based on a thorough and un-
    37	biased synthesis of the evidence.
    38	Reason 3: Updates. Research artifacts are essential for
    39	updating SRs. Updating SRs ensures that the evidence
    40	remains current, preventing the research from becoming
    41	outdated or misleading [6].
    42	Reason 4: Pathway to Automation. Writing an SR
    43	requires extensive time and effort – on average, 67
    44	weeks [7]. Human errors may occur in the SR process due
    45	to reduced vigilance. Automation can help both in catch-
    46	ing errors and reducing effort. Yet, research artifacts are
    47	needed to develop and validate the quality of automation.
    48	Recently, large language models (LLMs) have been used
    49	to automate SRs in SE by using existing research artifacts
    50	[8, 9].arXiv:2504.12646v3  [cs.SE]  16 Apr 2026
    51
    52	--- Page 2 ---
    53	2. Methods
    54	We conducted a systematic mapping study following
    55	the guidelines by Petersen et al. [10] and the SIGSOFT
    56	Empirical Standards checklist1. Full details of our re-
    57	search methods are available in our research artifact.2.
    58	2.1. Search process
    59	The search was finalized on 2nd October 2024, using
    60	Scopus, which indexes metadata, including paper titles,
    61	abstracts, and keywords, from all reputable scientific pub-
    62	lishers. The following search query was used:
    63	(ISSN(0928-8910) OR ISSN(1382-3256) OR
    64	ISSN(0950-5849) OR ISSN(2047-7481) OR
    65	ISSN(0164-1212) OR ISSN(0947-3602) OR
    66	ISSN(1619-1366) OR ISSN(0963-9314) OR
    67	ISSN(0960-0833) OR ISSN(0740-7459) OR
    68	ISSN(0038-0644) OR ISSN(1049-331x) OR
    69	ISSN(0098-5589) OR ISSN(0360-0300) OR
    70	ISSN(1876-7745) OR ISSN(1574-0137)) AND
    71	TITLE("Mapping Study" OR "Systematic review"
    72	OR "Systematic Literature Review" OR
    73	"Systematic Mapping" OR "Meta Analysis" OR
    74	"Meta Synthesis" OR "Scoping Review" OR
    75	"Case Survey" OR "Critical review") AND
    76	PUBYEAR > 2012 AND PUBYEAR < 2024
    77	We searched for papers from 13 well-known SE-related
    78	journals, and from two reputable journals known for pub-
    79	lishing reviews in the broader field of computer science.
    80	All selected journals are shown in Table 1a.
    81	We selected publications from 2013 to 2023, as key
    82	data repositories such as Zenodo and Figshare were
    83	launched in 2013 and 2011, respectively. Our search used
    84	keywords related to systematic literature reviews, system-
    85	atic mapping studies, meta-analysis and their correspond-
    86	ing variations, resulting in 643 articles.
    87	2.2. Study selection and quality assessment
    88	We applied the following inclusion criteria:
    89	•IC1: Study published between 2013–2023
    90	•IC2: Study is a secondary study
    91	•IC3: Study is related to SE
    92	1https://www2.sigsoft.org/EmpiricalStandards/tools/
    93	2https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.15488074Title–abstract screening eliminated studies that were
    94	not secondary studies (IC2) or related to SE (IC3).
    95	Research papers from “ACM Computing Surveys" and
    96	“Computer Science Review" journals were manually re-
    97	viewed by the authors to select SE-related studies, as the
    98	two journals are not strictly limited to SE-related publica-
    99	tions. After the manual review, the authors’ inter-rater re-
   100	liability was assessed using Krippendorff’s Alpha3. The
   101	resulting score (0.776 on a 95% confidence interval) in-
   102	dicates a strong agreement between the authors. After
   103	applying the inclusion criteria, 537 secondary studies re-
   104	mained.
   105	2.3. Data extraction
   106	Data extraction from the PDF files of secondary stud-
   107	ies was conducted in two rounds: (1) manually and (2)
   108	automatically. In the manual phase, full-text screening is
   109	conducted for each included article to identify dedicated
   110	sections indicating the availability of research artifacts. In
   111	the automated phase, keyword search is conducted for the
   112	articles with a Python script that prints 100 characters be-
   113	fore and after each keyword occurrence, which are then
   114	manually checked. We checked if the paper contains a ref-
   115	erence to the external resource and if it is located in a per-
   116	manent repository, such as Figshare, Zenodo or Mende-
   117	ley.
   118	3. Results
   119	The results are shown in Table 1.
   120	RQ1: What proportion of secondary studies include
   121	a research artifact?RQ1 gives an overview of the avail-
   122	ability of research artifacts in SE SRs. Ideally, every sec-
   123	ondary study would include a research artifact accessi-
   124	ble through a direct link. Results: In our dataset, 169
   125	of the 537 (31.5%) reviewed secondary studies include a
   126	research artifact.
   127	RQ2: Where are the research artifacts stored?RQ2
   128	addresses the storage location for research artifacts. Re-
   129	search artifacts should be located in permanent storage
   130	with a digital object identifier (DOI). Links to personal or
   131	institutional web pages may stop working in a few years.
